Alembic migrations.

## Creating a migration — ALWAYS use `alembic revision`

    uv run alembic revision --autogenerate -m "short description"
    # or, for a hand-written migration:
    uv run alembic revision -m "short description"

This generates a **random revision id** and a `YYYYMMDD_<rev>_<slug>.py` filename
automatically. Then edit the generated upgrade()/downgrade() and run it locally:

    uv run alembic upgrade head

DO NOT hand-author migration files, and DO NOT pick the revision id or a
sequential number yourself. The old convention of copying the previous file and
incrementing a `0014`-style counter + a hex id is what caused a production
incident: two PRs branched off the same head, both picked the same next id, and
`alembic upgrade head` failed with "revision present more than once / Multiple
head revisions", wedging the deploy. Random ids + `%(rev)s` filenames make that
impossible.

## Multiple heads (two migrations off the same parent)

If your branch and `main` each added a migration, after rebasing you'll have two
heads. That's expected — resolve it the standard Alembic way:

    uv run alembic heads          # shows >1 head
    uv run alembic merge heads -m "merge <a> and <b>"   # creates a merge migration

(Or, if your migration hasn't shipped yet, just re-point its `down_revision` to
main's current head.) CI fails the build on more than one head, so this is caught
in the PR, never in prod — see the "Alembic single head" step in
`.github/workflows/ci.yml`.

## Tests

Tests bypass migrations and use `Base.metadata.create_all(engine)` against an
in-memory SQLite — see `tests/conftest.py`. Postgres-only DDL (extensions, GIN
indexes) must therefore be guarded with a dialect check so the SQLite path is a
no-op (see the trigram-search-indexes migration).
